window.NCE_DATA = {"domains":{"1":"Professional Development & Counselor Self-Awareness","2":"Intake & Assessment","3":"Treatment Planning & Continuity of Care","4":"Provision of Counseling Interventions","5":"Indirect Client Care","6":"Legal & Ethical Compliance"},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"A client says, \"My manager criticized my report in front of the whole team, and I just froze.\" Which response is a reflection of feeling?","options":["\"Why didn't you speak up for yourself in the meeting?\"","\"You felt embarrassed and caught off guard in front of everyone.\"","\"Tell me more about your manager.\""],"answer":1,"domain":4,"rationale":"A reflection of feeling names the emotion underneath the client's words. B captures embarrassment and shock. A is a \"why\" question that can sound judgmental and puts the client on the defensive. C is an open invitation, but it shifts the focus to the manager rather than reflecting the client's feelings."},{"n":2,"stem":"Scores on a self-esteem inventory have a mean of 80 and a standard deviation of 5. A client scores 90. What is the client's z-score?","options":["1.0","10.0","2.0"],"answer":2,"domain":2,"rationale":"A z-score equals the raw score minus the mean, divided by the standard deviation: (90 − 80) ÷ 5 = 2.0. A would require a score of 85. B is the raw distance from the mean, not a standard score."},{"n":3,"stem":"In a state with a mandatory duty-to-protect law, a client describes a specific, credible plan to seriously harm his former partner, whom he names. What should the counselor do?","options":["Take the protective steps the state law requires, such as warning the partner or notifying law enforcement, and document the actions","Keep the statement confidential, because privilege belongs to the client","Wait until the next session to see whether the client still feels the same way"],"answer":0,"domain":6,"rationale":"A serious threat against a reasonably identifiable person triggers the duty to protect where state law requires it. The counselor follows the steps the law specifies and documents them. B confuses privilege, which applies to court testimony, with the duty to protect. C delays action when a third party is at risk."},{"n":4,"stem":"A newly licensed counselor is asked by her agency to lead a play therapy group for young children next week. She has no training in play therapy. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Lead the group, because her license allows her to work with any population","Lead the group after reading a book on play therapy over the weekend","Explain that she needs training and supervision in play therapy before leading the group, and help the agency consider other options"],"answer":2,"domain":1,"rationale":"Counselors practice only within the boundaries of their education, training, and supervised experience. New specialties require training and supervision first. A misunderstands what a license permits. B treats a weekend of reading as competence, which it is not."},{"n":5,"stem":"Which treatment plan objective is written most effectively?","options":["Client will use a paced-breathing skill in at least 4 of 5 stressful work situations per week, as recorded in a daily log, within six weeks","Client will feel less anxious at work","Client will develop a better understanding of anxiety"],"answer":0,"domain":3,"rationale":"A good objective is specific, measurable, achievable, realistic, and time-bound. A names the behavior, how often, how it will be measured, and by when. B and C are vague and cannot be measured."},{"n":6,"stem":"A counselor asks, \"On a scale from 0 to 10, where 10 means things are as good as they could be, where are you today? And what would it take to move up just one point?\" Which approach is the counselor using?","options":["Narrative therapy","Reality therapy","Solution-focused brief therapy"],"answer":2,"domain":4,"rationale":"Scaling questions are a signature technique of solution-focused brief therapy, developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg. They track progress and focus on small, achievable next steps. A focuses on externalizing problems and re-authoring stories. B uses the WDEP system to examine wants, behavior, and plans."},{"n":7,"stem":"An attorney in a custody dispute asks a counselor to testify about a current client and to give her expert opinion on which parent should receive custody. What is the most appropriate response?","options":["Agree to give the custody opinion, because she knows the family better than anyone else","Decline to act as a custody evaluator or expert, and limit any required testimony to her role as the treating counselor","Write a custody recommendation for the court instead of testifying in person"],"answer":1,"domain":6,"rationale":"A treating counselor is a fact witness who can describe direct observations. Giving a custody opinion is a forensic role, and combining treating and forensic roles creates a conflict that can harm the client and the counseling relationship. A combines the two roles. C still offers a forensic opinion, just in writing."},{"n":8,"stem":"A counselor working with a 7-year-old considers how the long, unpredictable shifts at his mother's workplace affect the boy, even though he never goes there. In Bronfenbrenner's ecological model, the mother's workplace is part of the child's","options":["exosystem","microsystem","macrosystem"],"answer":0,"domain":2,"rationale":"In Urie Bronfenbrenner's model, the exosystem includes settings the child does not take part in directly but that still affect him, such as a parent's workplace. B includes settings where the child participates directly, such as home and school. C is the broad cultural context of values, laws, and customs."},{"n":9,"stem":"A team that includes a 14-year-old, his parents, his teacher, his probation officer, and his counselor meets regularly to build one family-driven plan that coordinates services across all of these systems. What is this approach called?","options":["Partial hospitalization","Wraparound","Utilization review"],"answer":1,"domain":5,"rationale":"Wraparound is a team-based, family-driven planning process for people with complex needs who are involved with several systems. A is a level of care offering several hours of structured treatment each day. C is a payer's review of whether services are medically necessary."},{"n":10,"stem":"During supervision, a counselor-in-training avoids discussing a client's grief. The supervisor learns that the supervisee's mother died recently. In Bernard's discrimination model, which focus area does this concern most directly involve?","options":["Intervention","Conceptualization","Personalization"],"answer":2,"domain":1,"rationale":"In Bernard's discrimination model, personalization concerns how the counselor's own feelings, history, and reactions affect the work. A recent loss that leads the supervisee to avoid a client's grief is a personalization issue. A concerns observable skills. B concerns understanding the client's situation."},{"n":11,"stem":"A client says, \"I absolutely must get every question right on my licensing exam, or I'm a complete failure.\" A counselor using rational emotive behavior therapy would most likely","options":["dispute the irrational demand and the global self-rating","ask for the client's earliest childhood memory","invite the client to speak to an empty chair"],"answer":0,"domain":4,"rationale":"Albert Ellis's REBT targets rigid \"musts\" and global self-ratings and teaches clients to dispute them. B reflects Adlerian early recollections. C is a Gestalt technique."},{"n":12,"stem":"A counselor wants to know whether all the items on a new anxiety inventory measure the same construct. Which statistic best answers this question?","options":["A test-retest correlation","Cronbach's alpha","Cohen's kappa"],"answer":1,"domain":2,"rationale":"Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency, meaning how well the items hang together around a single construct. A measures stability over time. C measures agreement between raters."},{"n":13,"stem":"Three years after ending counseling, a counselor runs into a former client at a community event. Both are single, and the counselor would like to start dating the former client. According to the ACA Code of Ethics, what applies?","options":["The relationship is permitted, because counseling has ended","The relationship is permitted if the former client starts it","The relationship is prohibited, because the Code bars romantic relationships with former clients for five years"],"answer":2,"domain":6,"rationale":"The ACA Code prohibits sexual or romantic relationships with former clients for five years after the last professional contact. Even after five years, the counselor must show the relationship is not exploitive. A and B ignore the five-year rule."},{"n":14,"stem":"A client with an alcohol use disorder has stable housing, no risk of withdrawal, strong motivation, and a full-time job. Weekly sessions have not been enough to help him stay sober. Which level of care is most appropriate?","options":["Medically managed inpatient withdrawal management","An intensive outpatient program","Long-term residential treatment"],"answer":1,"domain":3,"rationale":"An intensive outpatient program adds structure and hours while letting the client keep his housing and job. A is for clients at risk of dangerous withdrawal. C is more restrictive than this client needs."},{"n":15,"stem":"A family counselor notices that a mother and her teenage son regularly side against the father. The counselor asks the parents to sit together, has them discuss a curfew while the son waits, and blocks the son from interrupting. Which approach is the counselor using?","options":["Structural family therapy","Bowen family systems therapy","Strategic family therapy"],"answer":0,"domain":4,"rationale":"Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy uses enactments, changes in seating, and boundary-making to strengthen the parental subsystem. B focuses on differentiation and multigenerational patterns, often with genograms. C relies on directives and strategies aimed at the problem behavior."},{"n":16,"stem":"A principal asks a school counselor to help two teachers manage a student who disrupts class. The counselor meets only with the teachers, helping them understand the student's behavior and refine their own classroom responses. In Caplan's model, this is","options":["consultee-centered case consultation","client-centered case consultation","program-centered administrative consultation"],"answer":0,"domain":1,"rationale":"In consultee-centered case consultation, the consultant helps consultees, here the teachers, handle a specific case more effectively. B would involve the consultant assessing the student and recommending a plan. C concerns designing or running a program."},{"n":17,"stem":"A new depression screening tool correctly identifies 90 percent of people who actually have depression. This figure describes the tool's","options":["specificity","predictive validity","sensitivity"],"answer":2,"domain":2,"rationale":"Sensitivity is the ability of a test to correctly identify people who have the condition. A is the ability to correctly rule out people who do not have it. B concerns how well scores predict a future outcome."},{"n":18,"stem":"A 15-year-old client's parents ask the counselor to tell them everything their son says in sessions. What is the best way for the counselor to handle confidentiality with minors?","options":["Tell the parents they have no right to any information","Balance the minor's privacy with the parents' legal rights and state law, ideally after agreeing with both on the limits of confidentiality at the start of counseling","Share everything the client says, word for word, because parents hold the legal rights"],"answer":1,"domain":6,"rationale":"Parents or guardians usually hold the legal rights in counseling a minor, but counselors protect the minor's privacy as far as possible. The best practice is to agree on the limits of confidentiality with everyone at the start and to follow state law. A ignores parental rights. C ignores the minor's welfare and the trust the relationship depends on."},{"n":19,"stem":"A counselor writes, \"Client reports sleeping about four hours a night and waking with racing thoughts.\" In a SOAP note, where does this statement belong?","options":["Assessment","Objective","Subjective"],"answer":2,"domain":5,"rationale":"The Subjective section records what the client reports, including symptoms in the client's own terms. A contains the counselor's clinical impressions. B contains observable data, such as appearance, behavior, or test scores."},{"n":20,"stem":"In the second month of a counseling group, members start challenging the leader and criticizing one another. What is the leader's best response?","options":["Ignore the conflict so the group stays positive","Name the conflict openly, invite members to talk about it, and model nondefensive responses","End the group early, because it is not working"],"answer":1,"domain":4,"rationale":"Conflict and challenges to the leader are normal in the transition or storming stage. A leader who names the conflict openly and responds without defensiveness helps the group build trust and move on to productive work. A leaves tension unspoken. C treats a normal developmental stage as failure."},{"n":21,"stem":"A client says, \"I know my drinking is hurting my family. I'm going to cut back starting next month, and I've already looked up a few support group meetings.\" Which stage of change best fits this client?","options":["Contemplation","Action","Preparation"],"answer":2,"domain":3,"rationale":"In the preparation stage, a person intends to act soon, usually within the next month, and has taken small steps. A describes awareness without a plan to act soon. B involves active, observable change already under way."},{"n":22,"stem":"A client insists, despite clear evidence to the contrary, that his neighbors have placed a device in his wall to read his thoughts. On a mental status examination, where is this recorded?","options":["Thought content","Thought process","Perception"],"answer":0,"domain":2,"rationale":"A fixed false belief is a delusion, recorded under thought content, which covers what the client thinks about. B describes how thoughts are organized and connected. C covers sensory experiences such as hallucinations."},{"n":23,"stem":"A counselor always calls clients back when she says she will and keeps every commitment she makes about scheduling. Which ethical principle does this behavior most clearly reflect?","options":["Fidelity","Veracity","Justice"],"answer":0,"domain":6,"rationale":"Fidelity means keeping promises and honoring commitments to clients. B means being truthful. C means treating people fairly and equitably."},{"n":24,"stem":"A counselor feels unusually angry and protective while a teenage client describes conflict with her strict father. The counselor's own father was harsh and controlling. What is the best first step?","options":["Refer the client to another counselor right away","Explore the reaction in supervision or consultation to manage the countertransference","Share her own story with the client to build rapport"],"answer":1,"domain":1,"rationale":"Strong reactions rooted in the counselor's own history are countertransference. The first step is to explore them in supervision or consultation so they do not harm the work. A is premature, because many reactions can be managed. C shifts the focus to the counselor and risks a boundary problem."},{"n":25,"stem":"In a recovery group, a member who has been sober for two years describes how he got through his first month. Several newer members say that, for the first time, they believe recovery might be possible for them. Which therapeutic factor are the newer members experiencing?","options":["Altruism","Catharsis","Instillation of hope"],"answer":2,"domain":4,"rationale":"Instillation of hope, one of Irvin Yalom's therapeutic factors, grows when members see others who have improved. A is the benefit that comes from helping others, which the experienced member, not the newer members, is more likely to gain. B is the release of strong emotion."},{"n":26,"stem":"A busy counselor usually writes her progress notes about two weeks after each session. What is the best practice?","options":["Complete each note promptly after the session, while the details are accurate","Write notes only when an insurer requests them","Keep brief notes in a personal notebook at home and type them up later"],"answer":0,"domain":5,"rationale":"Records should be accurate and timely. Notes written soon after a session are more reliable, support continuity of care if another provider steps in, and protect both client and counselor. B is wrong, because counselors keep records whether or not anyone requests them. C puts client information at risk outside a secure record system."},{"n":27,"stem":"A client describes four days last spring when she slept three hours a night, felt unusually energetic, and started several projects at once. Coworkers noticed the change, but she kept working and was never hospitalized. She also had a major depressive episode two years ago. Which diagnosis best fits?","options":["Bipolar I disorder","Bipolar II disorder","Cyclothymic disorder"],"answer":1,"domain":2,"rationale":"Four days of elevated energy and reduced need for sleep that others notice, without marked impairment or hospitalization, is a hypomanic episode. A hypomanic episode plus a past major depressive episode fits bipolar II disorder. A requires a full manic episode, lasting at least a week or leading to hospitalization. C requires two years of milder mood swings that never meet full episode criteria, and this client has had a full major depressive episode."},{"n":28,"stem":"A counselor's religious beliefs conflict with a client's goal of strengthening her same-sex relationship. According to the ACA Code of Ethics, what should the counselor do?","options":["Refer the client because of the values conflict","Gently encourage the client to reconsider her goals","Continue working toward the client's goals, avoid imposing personal values, and seek supervision or training as needed"],"answer":2,"domain":6,"rationale":"The ACA Code directs counselors not to impose their personal values and prohibits referral based only on the counselor's values. Counselors seek training or supervision when values conflict with a client's goals. A is a values-based referral. B imposes the counselor's values."},{"n":29,"stem":"A counselor will leave her agency in six weeks. What is the best way to handle her current clients?","options":["Tell clients early, discuss their options, and arrange transfers, using warm handoffs where possible","Tell clients at their final session","Leave all transfer decisions to the agency without discussing them with clients"],"answer":0,"domain":3,"rationale":"Early notice, collaborative planning, and warm handoffs protect continuity of care and prevent abandonment. B gives clients no time to prepare. C excludes clients from decisions about their own care."},{"n":30,"stem":"A client whose Holland code is strongly Social works alone all day as a data-entry clerk and feels drained and unfulfilled. In Holland's theory, her situation reflects low","options":["differentiation","congruence","consistency"],"answer":1,"domain":4,"rationale":"In John Holland's theory, congruence is the fit between a person's type and the work environment. A Social type in an isolated, data-focused job has low congruence, which predicts lower satisfaction. A refers to how clearly one type stands out in a person's profile. C refers to how closely related a person's top types are on the RIASEC hexagon."},{"n":31,"stem":"In the second session, a White counselor says to a Black client, \"I'm aware that we come from different racial backgrounds. I'm wondering how that feels for you as we talk about what's happening at your job.\" What is the counselor doing?","options":["Using self-disclosure to build rapport","Imposing personal values","Broaching"],"answer":2,"domain":1,"rationale":"Broaching is a counselor's intentional invitation to discuss cultural factors, such as race, that may matter to the client or to the counseling relationship. A shares personal information for the client's benefit, but the counselor's aim here is to open a conversation about culture. B does not apply, because the counselor imposes no beliefs or values."},{"n":32,"stem":"Under HIPAA, which statement about psychotherapy notes is accurate?","options":["They are the same as progress notes and are part of the regular medical record","They are kept separate from the medical record and receive additional protection","Insurers may obtain them whenever they request them"],"answer":1,"domain":6,"rationale":"Under HIPAA, psychotherapy notes are a counselor's separately kept notes analyzing session content, and they receive extra protection. A confuses them with progress notes. C is wrong, because insurers generally cannot demand psychotherapy notes as a condition of payment."},{"n":33,"stem":"The family of an 82-year-old man reports that over the past two days he has become confused, with attention that comes and goes and worsens at night. What should the counselor do first?","options":["Begin cognitive behavioral therapy for late-life depression","Schedule a neuropsychological evaluation in six months","Arrange an urgent medical evaluation for possible delirium"],"answer":2,"domain":2,"rationale":"Sudden onset over hours to days, fluctuating attention, and worsening at night point to delirium, which needs urgent medical evaluation. A skips the medical emergency. B fits a gradual neurocognitive decline, not an acute change."},{"n":34,"stem":"A client started an SSRI antidepressant two weeks ago. She says she feels no better yet and plans to stop taking it tomorrow. What is the counselor's best response?","options":["Agree that she should stop, because the medication is clearly not working","Explain that antidepressants often take several weeks to show full benefit, and encourage her to talk with her prescriber before making any change","Suggest that she ask her doctor to switch her to a benzodiazepine instead"],"answer":1,"domain":5,"rationale":"Antidepressants such as SSRIs often take several weeks to reach their full effect, and medication decisions belong to the client and her prescriber. Counselors give accurate general information and encourage communication with the prescriber. A is outside a counselor's scope, and stopping abruptly can cause problems. C is also outside the counselor's scope, and benzodiazepines are not a substitute treatment for depression."},{"n":35,"stem":"A counselor is using Roberts' seven-stage crisis intervention model with a client in acute distress. What is the first stage?","options":["Developing and formulating an action plan","Arranging follow-up","Planning and conducting a thorough biopsychosocial and lethality assessment"],"answer":2,"domain":4,"rationale":"Albert Roberts' model begins with a thorough biopsychosocial and lethality assessment, so the counselor knows whether the client is in immediate danger. A comes later, after rapport, problem identification, and exploring feelings and alternatives. B is the final stage."},{"n":36,"stem":"A counselor records a client's daily panic attacks for two weeks without treatment, introduces an intervention, withdraws it, and then reintroduces it. Which research design is this?","options":["AB design","ABAB (reversal) design","Multiple-baseline design across settings"],"answer":1,"domain":3,"rationale":"An ABAB design records a baseline (A), introduces treatment (B), withdraws it (A), and reintroduces it (B). If the behavior changes each time, the case for the intervention is stronger. A stops after the first treatment phase. C staggers the start of treatment across settings without withdrawing it."},{"n":37,"stem":"A client of a counselor licensed in Ohio moves to Arizona for three months and asks to continue sessions by video. What should the counselor do?","options":["Check the laws of Arizona, where the client will be located, including licensure or Counseling Compact privilege, before continuing","Continue without change, because only the counselor's home-state license matters","Switch to email sessions, because licensure rules do not apply to email"],"answer":0,"domain":6,"rationale":"In telehealth, the laws of the state where the client is physically located generally apply. The counselor must hold authorization to practice there, such as a license or a Counseling Compact privilege to practice. B ignores the client's location. C is wrong, because licensure rules apply to every form of counseling, including email."},{"n":38,"stem":"A graduate student wants to make sure her master's program meets the most widely recognized accreditation standards for counselor education. Which organization accredits counselor education programs?","options":["NBCC","CACREP","ACA"],"answer":1,"domain":1,"rationale":"The Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP) accredits counselor education programs. A, the National Board for Certified Counselors, offers national certification (the NCC) and develops the NCE. C, the American Counseling Association, is the profession's largest membership association and publishes the ACA Code of Ethics."},{"n":39,"stem":"During a risk assessment, a client with recent suicidal thoughts mentions that her late husband's handgun is still in her bedside drawer. What is the counselor's best step?","options":["Work with the client on ways to reduce access to the firearm during this period, such as storing it with a trusted person","Avoid the topic, because firearm ownership is a personal matter","Tell the client she must sell the gun before her next session"],"answer":0,"domain":2,"rationale":"Lethal means counseling, working with the client to reduce access to highly lethal methods during a period of risk, is a key part of safety planning. A collaborative approach, such as temporary storage with someone she trusts, is more likely to be followed. B ignores a serious risk factor. C is a demand rather than a collaborative plan, and it may damage trust and lead the client to stop being open."},{"n":40,"stem":"A client says, \"Part of me wants to quit smoking, but it's the only way I know how to relax.\" Which response best reflects motivational interviewing?","options":["\"You really need to quit. Smoking is going to make you sick.\"","\"On one hand, smoking helps you relax, and on the other hand, you're thinking about quitting.\"","\"Why haven't you quit already?\""],"answer":1,"domain":4,"rationale":"A double-sided reflection captures both sides of the client's ambivalence, a core motivational interviewing skill. A is the righting reflex, which tends to increase resistance. C is a \"why\" question that can feel like blame."},{"n":41,"stem":"A 9-year-old client tells her school counselor that her father hit her with a belt last night, and the counselor sees bruises on her arm. What should the counselor do?","options":["Investigate further to confirm the abuse before taking action","Call the father to hear his side of the story first","Report the suspected abuse to child protective services as required by law"],"answer":2,"domain":6,"rationale":"Counselors must report reasonable suspicion of child abuse. They do not need proof. A goes beyond the counselor's role, because investigation belongs to child protective services. B could put the child at greater risk."},{"n":42,"stem":"After eight sessions, a client's scores on a weekly outcome measure show no improvement. What should the counselor do?","options":["Continue the current plan unchanged, because change takes time","End counseling, because it is not working","Review progress with the client and revise the goals or interventions together"],"answer":2,"domain":3,"rationale":"Measurement-based care uses outcome data to guide decisions, and a lack of progress calls for a collaborative review and revision of the plan. A ignores the data. B is premature and may leave the client without help."},{"n":43,"stem":"Which CPT code describes a psychiatric diagnostic evaluation?","options":["90791","90834","90853"],"answer":0,"domain":5,"rationale":"CPT 90791 is the psychiatric diagnostic evaluation without medical services. B is a 45-minute (38 to 52 minutes) individual psychotherapy session. C is group psychotherapy."},{"n":44,"stem":"Which feature best distinguishes clinical supervision from consultation?","options":["Supervision is evaluative and hierarchical, while consultation is voluntary and nonhierarchical","Consultation includes a gatekeeping role, while supervision does not","Supervision focuses on one case at a time, while consultation always covers the whole caseload"],"answer":0,"domain":1,"rationale":"Supervision is an ongoing, evaluative, hierarchical relationship that protects clients and serves a gatekeeping role for the profession. Consultation is a voluntary, nonhierarchical relationship focused on a specific professional issue, and the consultee is free to accept or reject the advice. B reverses the two, because gatekeeping belongs to supervision. C has no basis, since either relationship can address one case or many."},{"n":45,"stem":"A licensing board compares each candidate's score with a fixed passing standard rather than with the scores of other candidates. This approach is","options":["norm-referenced","ipsative","criterion-referenced"],"answer":2,"domain":2,"rationale":"A criterion-referenced approach compares each score with a fixed standard, which is how passing scores on licensing exams work. A compares scores with those of a norm group. B compares a person's scores with their own other scores."},{"n":46,"stem":"A counselor is treating a client with PTSD after a car crash using prolonged exposure. Which pair of techniques is central to this approach?","options":["Imaginal exposure and in vivo exposure","Bilateral stimulation and resource installation","Externalizing and re-authoring"],"answer":0,"domain":4,"rationale":"Edna Foa's prolonged exposure uses imaginal exposure, which means revisiting the trauma memory, and in vivo exposure, which means facing avoided situations in real life. B describes EMDR. C describes narrative therapy."},{"n":47,"stem":"A counselor learns that a colleague is billing insurance for sessions that never took place. When the counselor raises it privately, the colleague dismisses the concern. What should the counselor do next?","options":["Report the conduct to the appropriate licensing board or ethics committee","Take no further action, because informal resolution was attempted","Contact the colleague's clients to warn them"],"answer":0,"domain":6,"rationale":"When informal resolution fails and the misconduct is serious, such as insurance fraud, counselors take further action by reporting to the appropriate board or ethics committee. B leaves serious harm unaddressed. C could breach confidentiality and is not an appropriate step."},{"n":48,"stem":"A client is about to finish an intensive outpatient program. Which step best supports continuity of care?","options":["Closing the record without contacting the next provider","An aftercare plan with a scheduled follow-up appointment and relapse prevention supports","A general recommendation to \"keep up the good work\""],"answer":1,"domain":3,"rationale":"A specific aftercare plan with a scheduled appointment and relapse prevention supports bridges the move from one level of care to the next. A breaks continuity. C is too vague to support recovery."},{"n":49,"stem":"A counselor at a primary care clinic walks a patient down the hall and personally introduces her to the clinic's substance use specialist. This is an example of","options":["a cold referral","consultee-centered consultation","a warm handoff"],"answer":2,"domain":5,"rationale":"A warm handoff happens when the counselor directly connects the client with the new provider, which raises the chance the client follows through. A gives the client a name or number and leaves the next step to them. B is a consultation relationship between professionals."},{"n":50,"stem":"A counselor notices that she checks work email late every night and dreads Monday mornings. How does the ACA Code of Ethics frame self-care?","options":["As a personal choice with no professional implications","As a professional responsibility, because counselor impairment can harm clients","As important only after a client files a complaint"],"answer":1,"domain":1,"rationale":"The ACA Code treats self-care as part of professional responsibility, because burnout and impairment can reduce the quality of care and harm clients. A misses that link. C waits until harm has already occurred."}],"decks":[{"name":"Key Terms","cards":[{"front":"Abandonment","back":"Ending counseling inappropriately, without notice, referral, or attention to client needs."},{"front":"Abstinence violation effect","back":"Guilt and all-or-nothing thinking after a lapse that can lead to full relapse."},{"front":"Accommodation (Piaget)","back":"Changing existing mental structures to fit new information."},{"front":"Aftercare plan","back":"Services and supports arranged to follow discharge from a level of care."},{"front":"Assent","back":"Agreement to services by a person who cannot give legal consent, such as a minor."},{"front":"Assimilation (Piaget)","back":"Fitting new information into existing mental structures."},{"front":"Bracketing","back":"Setting aside one's own assumptions to understand another person's experience."},{"front":"Broaching","back":"A counselor's intentional invitation to discuss cultural factors relevant to the client or relationship."},{"front":"Burnout","back":"Exhaustion, cynicism, and reduced efficacy from chronic workplace stress."},{"front":"Business associate agreement (BAA)","back":"A HIPAA contract requiring vendors that handle PHI to protect it."},{"front":"Case conceptualization","back":"An organized explanation of a client's difficulties that guides treatment."},{"front":"Collateral information","back":"Information about a client from sources other than the client."},{"front":"Compassion fatigue","back":"Exhaustion from caring for suffering people, often combining burnout and secondary traumatic stress."},{"front":"Confidentiality","back":"The counselor's ethical and legal duty to protect client information."},{"front":"Congruence","back":"Genuineness; consistency between the counselor's inner experience and outward expression."},{"front":"Consultation","back":"A voluntary, nonhierarchical relationship focused on a specific professional issue."},{"front":"Contraindication","back":"A reason not to use a particular intervention with a particular client."},{"front":"Countertransference","back":"A counselor's emotional reactions to a client rooted in the counselor's own history."},{"front":"CPT code","back":"A code describing a health care service for billing."},{"front":"Cultural concept of distress","back":"A culturally specific way of experiencing, expressing, or explaining distress."},{"front":"Differentiation of self","back":"Bowen's term for remaining connected to family while maintaining one's own thoughts and feelings."},{"front":"Discharge plan","back":"A document describing the end of an episode of care, progress, needs, and next steps."},{"front":"Duty to protect","back":"A legal obligation in some states to take reasonable steps to protect identifiable people from a client's serious threats."},{"front":"Empathy","back":"Understanding a client's inner world as if it were one's own and communicating that understanding."},{"front":"Evidence-based practice","back":"Integrating research, clinical expertise, and client characteristics and preferences."},{"front":"Externalizing","back":"A narrative technique that separates the person from the problem."},{"front":"Fact witness","back":"A witness who testifies about direct observations."},{"front":"Formative evaluation","back":"Evaluation during a program or process to improve it."},{"front":"Genogram","back":"A multigenerational family diagram."},{"front":"Informed consent","back":"The process of giving clients the information needed to make free decisions about services."},{"front":"Lapse","back":"A single slip back into an old behavior."},{"front":"Lethal means counseling","back":"Working with clients to reduce access to highly lethal methods during periods of risk."},{"front":"Level of care","back":"The intensity and setting of services, from prevention to inpatient care."},{"front":"Linking","back":"A group skill that connects members' experiences."},{"front":"Medical necessity","back":"The standard insurers use to determine whether services are needed to treat a condition."},{"front":"Mental status examination","back":"A structured description of current mental functioning."},{"front":"Minimum necessary","back":"Sharing only the information needed for a specific purpose."},{"front":"Parallel process","back":"Dynamics from counseling appearing in supervision, or the reverse."},{"front":"Privileged communication","back":"Legal protection of client communications from disclosure in court; belongs to the client."},{"front":"Professional will","back":"A document naming a colleague to manage clients and records if a counselor dies or is incapacitated."},{"front":"Psychotherapy notes","back":"Under HIPAA, separately kept notes analyzing session content, with extra protection."},{"front":"Relapse","back":"A return to a previous pattern of behavior."},{"front":"Reliability","back":"Consistency of measurement."},{"front":"Rupture","back":"A strain or breakdown in the therapeutic alliance."},{"front":"Safety plan","back":"A written, collaborative plan a client can use during a suicidal crisis."},{"front":"Secondary traumatic stress","back":"Trauma-like symptoms from exposure to others' trauma."},{"front":"SMART goal","back":"A goal that is specific, measurable, attainable, realistic, and timely."},{"front":"Standard error of measurement","back":"An estimate of the spread of observed scores around a true score."},{"front":"Subpoena","back":"A legal document requiring testimony or documents; not the same as a court order."},{"front":"Summative evaluation","back":"Evaluation at the end of a program to judge its overall effectiveness."},{"front":"Supervision","back":"An evaluative, hierarchical, ongoing relationship that protects clients and develops counselors."},{"front":"Transference","back":"A client's redirection of feelings from past relationships onto the counselor."},{"front":"Triangulation","back":"Pulling a third person into a two-person conflict."},{"front":"Unconditional positive regard","back":"Accepting and valuing a client without judgment."},{"front":"Universality","back":"The group factor of realizing others share similar struggles."},{"front":"Validity","back":"The degree to which evidence supports score interpretations for an intended purpose."},{"front":"Vicarious trauma","back":"Lasting changes in a counselor's beliefs and worldview from empathic engagement with trauma."},{"front":"Warm handoff","back":"A referral in which the counselor directly connects the client with the new provider."},{"front":"Wraparound","back":"A team-based, family-driven planning process for people with complex needs across systems."}]},{"name":"Theorists and Theories","cards":[{"front":"Sigmund Freud","back":"**Psychoanalysis.** Id, ego, superego; unconscious; psychosexual stages; defense mechanisms. *Key techniques or terms:* Free association, dream analysis, interpretation, transference."},{"front":"Carl Jung","back":"**Analytical psychology.** Collective unconscious, archetypes, persona, shadow, individuation. *Key techniques or terms:* Dream work, exploration of archetypes."},{"front":"Alfred Adler","back":"**Individual psychology.** Social interest, inferiority, lifestyle, birth order, private logic. *Key techniques or terms:* Early recollections, encouragement, acting as if, spitting in the soup."},{"front":"Carl Rogers","back":"**Person-centered.** Actualizing tendency, conditions of worth, incongruence. *Key techniques or terms:* Empathy, unconditional positive regard, congruence."},{"front":"Viktor Frankl","back":"**Logotherapy (existential).** Will to meaning, meaning in suffering. *Key techniques or terms:* Paradoxical intention, dereflection."},{"front":"Rollo May, Irvin Yalom","back":"**Existential therapy.** Death, freedom, isolation, meaninglessness. *Key techniques or terms:* Exploring responsibility and meaning."},{"front":"Fritz and Laura Perls","back":"**Gestalt therapy.** Here and now, awareness, unfinished business, contact boundaries. *Key techniques or terms:* Empty chair, two-chair, exaggeration."},{"front":"Ivan Pavlov, John B. Watson","back":"**Classical conditioning.** Learned associations between stimuli. *Key techniques or terms:* Conditioning, extinction."},{"front":"B. F. Skinner","back":"**Operant conditioning.** Reinforcement, punishment, schedules. *Key techniques or terms:* Token economies, shaping."},{"front":"Joseph Wolpe","back":"**Behavior therapy.** Reciprocal inhibition. *Key techniques or terms:* Systematic desensitization."},{"front":"Albert Bandura","back":"**Social learning (social cognitive) theory.** Modeling, self-efficacy, reciprocal determinism. *Key techniques or terms:* Modeling, behavioral rehearsal."},{"front":"Albert Ellis","back":"**Rational emotive behavior therapy.** ABC model, irrational beliefs, \"musts,\" unconditional self-acceptance. *Key techniques or terms:* Disputing (ABCDE)."},{"front":"Aaron Beck","back":"**Cognitive therapy / CBT.** Automatic thoughts, schemas, cognitive triad, distortions. *Key techniques or terms:* Socratic questioning, thought records, behavioral experiments."},{"front":"Donald Meichenbaum","back":"**Cognitive behavior modification.** Self-talk. *Key techniques or terms:* Stress inoculation, self-instructional training."},{"front":"William Glasser, Robert Wubbolding","back":"**Reality therapy / choice theory.** Five basic needs, quality world, responsibility. *Key techniques or terms:* WDEP, planning."},{"front":"Steve de Shazer, Insoo Kim Berg","back":"**Solution-focused brief therapy.** Exceptions, client strengths, small changes. *Key techniques or terms:* Miracle, exception, scaling, coping questions."},{"front":"Michael White, David Epston","back":"**Narrative therapy.** Problem-saturated stories, re-authoring. *Key techniques or terms:* Externalizing, unique outcomes."},{"front":"William Miller, Stephen Rollnick","back":"**Motivational interviewing.** Ambivalence, change talk, spirit of MI. *Key techniques or terms:* OARS, rolling with discord, decisional balance."},{"front":"Marsha Linehan","back":"**Dialectical behavior therapy.** Acceptance and change; biosocial theory. *Key techniques or terms:* Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness."},{"front":"Steven Hayes","back":"**Acceptance and commitment therapy.** Psychological flexibility. *Key techniques or terms:* Defusion, acceptance, values, committed action."},{"front":"Segal, Williams, Teasdale","back":"**Mindfulness-based cognitive therapy.** Relapse prevention for recurrent depression. *Key techniques or terms:* Mindfulness practices with cognitive principles."},{"front":"Cohen, Mannarino, Deblinger","back":"**Trauma-focused CBT.** Child trauma with caregiver involvement. *Key techniques or terms:* PRACTICE components."},{"front":"Patricia Resick","back":"**Cognitive processing therapy.** Stuck points about trauma. *Key techniques or terms:* Challenging beliefs; trauma themes."},{"front":"Edna Foa","back":"**Prolonged exposure.** Emotional processing of trauma. *Key techniques or terms:* Imaginal and in vivo exposure."},{"front":"Francine Shapiro","back":"**EMDR.** Adaptive information processing. *Key techniques or terms:* Bilateral stimulation, eight phases."},{"front":"Richard Schwartz","back":"**Internal family systems.** Self and parts (managers, firefighters, exiles). *Key techniques or terms:* Parts work."},{"front":"Murray Bowen","back":"**Family systems (multigenerational).** Differentiation, triangles, emotional cutoff, multigenerational transmission. *Key techniques or terms:* Genogram, detriangling."},{"front":"Salvador Minuchin","back":"**Structural family therapy.** Subsystems, boundaries, hierarchy. *Key techniques or terms:* Joining, enactments, restructuring."},{"front":"Jay Haley, Cloé Madanes","back":"**Strategic family therapy.** Power, hierarchy, attempted solutions. *Key techniques or terms:* Directives, paradox, ordeals."},{"front":"Virginia Satir","back":"**Experiential / human validation.** Self-esteem, communication stances. *Key techniques or terms:* Family sculpting."},{"front":"Carl Whitaker","back":"**Symbolic-experiential therapy.** Spontaneity, growth. *Key techniques or terms:* Use of self, absurdity."},{"front":"Sue Johnson","back":"**Emotionally focused therapy.** Attachment, negative interaction cycles. *Key techniques or terms:* Accessing and reshaping emotions."},{"front":"John and Julie Gottman","back":"**Gottman method.** Four Horsemen, Sound Relationship House. *Key techniques or terms:* Gentle start-up, repair attempts."},{"front":"Irvin Yalom","back":"**Group psychotherapy.** Therapeutic factors. *Key techniques or terms:* Here-and-now process."},{"front":"Bruce Tuckman","back":"**Group development.** Forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning. *Key techniques or terms:* Stage-matched leadership."},{"front":"Frank Parsons","back":"**Trait-and-factor career theory.** Self-knowledge, knowledge of work, true reasoning. *Key techniques or terms:* Matching."},{"front":"John Holland","back":"**Vocational personalities.** RIASEC, congruence, consistency, differentiation, identity. *Key techniques or terms:* Self-Directed Search."},{"front":"Donald Super","back":"**Life-span, life-space.** Self-concept, life stages, life-career rainbow. *Key techniques or terms:* Career maturity and adaptability."},{"front":"John Krumboltz","back":"**Social learning theory of career decision making.** Genetic endowment, environment, learning experiences, task approach skills. *Key techniques or terms:* Planned happenstance."},{"front":"Linda Gottfredson","back":"**Circumscription and compromise.** Narrowing by sex type and prestige. *Key techniques or terms:* Exploring eliminated options."},{"front":"Lent, Brown, Hackett","back":"**Social cognitive career theory.** Self-efficacy, outcome expectations, goals. *Key techniques or terms:* Addressing barriers and supports."},{"front":"Mark Savickas","back":"**Career construction.** Life themes, career adaptability. *Key techniques or terms:* Career story interview."},{"front":"Erik Erikson","back":"**Psychosocial development.** Eight stages and crises. *Key techniques or terms:* Stage-based understanding."},{"front":"Jean Piaget","back":"**Cognitive development.** Schemas, assimilation, accommodation; four stages. *Key techniques or terms:* Developmentally matched interventions."},{"front":"Lawrence Kohlberg","back":"**Moral development.** Preconventional, conventional, postconventional. *Key techniques or terms:* Moral dilemmas."},{"front":"Carol Gilligan","back":"**Ethic of care.** Care-based moral reasoning. *Key techniques or terms:* Relational focus."},{"front":"John Bowlby, Mary Ainsworth","back":"**Attachment theory.** Secure base; secure, avoidant, resistant, disorganized. *Key techniques or terms:* Strange Situation."},{"front":"Urie Bronfenbrenner","back":"**Ecological systems.** Micro, meso, exo, macro, chrono systems. *Key techniques or terms:* Contextual assessment."},{"front":"Prochaska, DiClemente","back":"**Transtheoretical model.** Stages of change. *Key techniques or terms:* Stage-matched interventions."},{"front":"Marlatt, Gordon","back":"**Relapse prevention.** High-risk situations, abstinence violation effect. *Key techniques or terms:* Coping plans."},{"front":"Kübler-Ross; Worden; Stroebe and Schut","back":"**Grief models.** Stages (not linear); tasks of mourning; dual process. *Key techniques or terms:* Grief counseling."},{"front":"Gerald Caplan","back":"**Mental health consultation.** Client- and consultee-centered, case and administrative. *Key techniques or terms:* Consultation types."},{"front":"Janine Bernard","back":"**Discrimination model of supervision.** Teacher, counselor, consultant roles. *Key techniques or terms:* Intervention, conceptualization, personalization."},{"front":"Stoltenberg and colleagues","back":"**Integrated Developmental Model.** Levels 1–3 of supervisee growth. *Key techniques or terms:* Developmentally matched supervision."}]},{"name":"Statistics and Assessment","cards":[{"front":"Nominal scale","back":"Categories with names only (diagnosis type)"},{"front":"Ordinal scale","back":"Ranked order without equal spacing (class rank)"},{"front":"Interval scale","back":"Equal spacing, no true zero (Fahrenheit)"},{"front":"Ratio scale","back":"Equal spacing with a true zero (number of sessions)"},{"front":"Mean","back":"Average; pulled toward extreme scores"},{"front":"Median","back":"Middle score"},{"front":"Mode","back":"Most frequent score"},{"front":"Positive skew","back":"Tail to the right; mean > median > mode; hard test"},{"front":"Negative skew","back":"Tail to the left; mean < median < mode; easy test"},{"front":"Range","back":"Highest minus lowest score"},{"front":"Variance","back":"Average squared distance from the mean"},{"front":"Standard deviation","back":"Typical distance from the mean"},{"front":"Normal curve","back":"About 68% within ±1 SD, 95% within ±2 SD, 99.7% within ±3 SD"},{"front":"Percentile rank","back":"Percent of norm group scoring at or below a score; not percent correct"},{"front":"z-score","back":"Mean 0, SD 1"},{"front":"T-score","back":"Mean 50, SD 10"},{"front":"Deviation IQ","back":"Mean 100, SD 15"},{"front":"Stanine","back":"Nine bands; mean 5, SD about 2"},{"front":"Correlation (r)","back":"Strength and direction of a relationship, −1 to +1; not causation"},{"front":"Coefficient of determination","back":"r squared; shared variance"},{"front":"Reliability","back":"Consistency of scores"},{"front":"Test-retest reliability","back":"Stability over time"},{"front":"Alternate forms reliability","back":"Equivalence of two versions"},{"front":"Split-half reliability","back":"Agreement of two halves; Spearman-Brown correction"},{"front":"Internal consistency","back":"Items measure the same thing; Cronbach's alpha, KR-20"},{"front":"Inter-rater reliability","back":"Agreement between scorers; Cohen's kappa"},{"front":"Standard error of measurement","back":"Expected spread of observed scores around the true score; ±1 SEM ≈ 68%, ±2 SEM ≈ 95%"},{"front":"Validity","back":"Accuracy of score interpretations for a purpose"},{"front":"Content validity","back":"Items cover the content domain"},{"front":"Concurrent validity","back":"Relates to a criterion measured now"},{"front":"Predictive validity","back":"Predicts a future criterion"},{"front":"Construct validity","back":"Measures the theoretical trait; includes convergent and discriminant evidence"},{"front":"Face validity","back":"Looks like it measures the trait; not true validity"},{"front":"Sensitivity","back":"Correctly identifies people who have the condition"},{"front":"Specificity","back":"Correctly rules out people who do not have the condition"},{"front":"Norm-referenced","back":"Compared with a norm group"},{"front":"Criterion-referenced","back":"Compared with a fixed standard"},{"front":"Ipsative","back":"Compared with the person's own other scores"},{"front":"Speed vs. power test","back":"Many easy items under time pressure vs. harder items with ample time"},{"front":"Independent variable","back":"What the researcher manipulates or groups by"},{"front":"Dependent variable","back":"The outcome measured"},{"front":"Internal validity","back":"Confidence that the intervention caused the change"},{"front":"External validity","back":"Generalizability of results"},{"front":"Type I error","back":"False positive; rejecting a true null hypothesis"},{"front":"Type II error","back":"False negative; missing a real effect"},{"front":"Alpha level","back":"Probability of Type I error, often 0.05"},{"front":"Power","back":"Probability of detecting a real effect; increases with sample size"},{"front":"Effect size","back":"Size of an effect; Cohen's d ≈ 0.2 small, 0.5 medium, 0.8 large"},{"front":"t-test","back":"Compares two means"},{"front":"ANOVA","back":"Compares three or more means"},{"front":"Chi-square","back":"Compares frequencies in categories"},{"front":"Regression","back":"Predicts an outcome from predictors"},{"front":"Formative evaluation","back":"Feedback during a program to improve it"},{"front":"Summative evaluation","back":"Judgment of overall effectiveness at the end"},{"front":"Needs assessment","back":"Identifies gaps before designing a program"}]}]};
